 "poem": "Cricket-filled, where it pitched again,\nThe castle parades.  The battle battle\nWould never be done for, again.\nVIII.  AURORE'S ACCOUNT about\nTHE BATTLE OF LATEENFIELD. Again\nShe spake; but his eyes were fixed again,\nAnd he could not picture again\nThe ambuscade of defeated.\nIX.  THOMAS FARALLIS'T BARON then\nREFUSED to join the battle again,\nTo the church-yard in the city, where\nHe now rested all day long, alone;\nHis master's leaving again.\nX.  HURRY!  Hurry, Uncle!\nHurry, Madam!  I will follow,\nI will follow and follow, dear!\nYea! oh, may I see again\nThese lives and faces again!\nHush! hush, my lassie!  Oh, don't worry,\nFor in the grassy vale before me,",
